prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 4–8 sentences: a coherent implementation slice is finished. What will you do to close the agent-dev loop?
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers the four gates in order: (1) Quality Gate — format, lint, type-check, self-review with a 0–100 score against a default threshold of 70; (2) Test Gate — run tests, fix-and-retry within a budget (default 2), refuse to push on continued failure; (3) Push Gate — push the feature branch to remote, with a pre-push grep for internal markers when in public-repo mode; (4) Review Gate — open or update the PR/CR. The Review Gate covers: refuse-on-fail if prior gates failed; resolve the review tool by agent's implicit knowledge for the host, then a `## Agent Development` → `kdevkit` block in project.md, then asking once and offering to persist; prepare title (Conventional Commits) + body (Why + Approach required; Verification / Reading guide / Pairs with optional); rerun the internal-marker grep against title+body before submission; check for an existing review on this branch and update if found, else create; return the URL. Wrong answers: skipping any gate; auto-merging or auto-closing; treating the body shape as fixed-five-sections; not checking for an existing PR before creating; running close-out / merge / branch deletion as part of this loop.
tools: claude,kiro
